                <head>14. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On November 4, 1977, President Jimmy
                        Carter wrote Soviet General Secretary Leonid Brezhnev that Soviet anti-satellite
                        (ASAT) testing in space had become “of
                    increasing concern to us” and had been “carefully noted in our country.”
                        Carter warned that this concern “has
                    emerged in our efforts to build support in the U.S. Senate for a SALT agreement. This is a seriously destabilizing
                    development which we have voluntarily foregone, although we have the technical
                    capability to build systems. A very early joint agreement not to conduct further
                    tests and to forego this capability,” the President said, “would be helpful.”
                    (Letter from Carter to Brezhnev, November 4; Carter Library, National
                    Security Affairs, Staff Material, Office, Outside the System File, Box 69,
                        USSR: Brezhnev-Carter
                    Correspondence: 6–12/77. Brezhnev replied that he was “ready to hold consultations” on
                        ASAT systems, but said the discussions
                    “would include, of course, all the systems and means which possess such
                    potential capability including manned space shuttles.” (Letter from Brezhnev to Carter, November 15; Carter
                    Library, Plains File, President’s Personal Foreign Affairs File, Box 4, USSR (Brezhnev Drafts/Letters) 4/77–9/80)</p>
            </div>
